ble power consumption curve, whereas Rowan's high memory usage might correlate with higher peak power draw during the prompt processing phase.\n\nThe practical next step is to conduct a controlled pilot test using a representative dataset that includes a mix of short and long prompts to stress-test the memory limits of Rowan and the generation consistency of Juniper. During this test, the team should record the exact time spent in each of the three phases for each engine under identical environmental conditions. Based on the results, the team can then select the optimal engine for the primary use case, or implement a hybrid approach where Cedar handles the initial setup and Juniper takes over for the generation phase, thereby mitigating the slow decoding issue while maintaining the steady generation rate."}
