part of the evidence, not a guarantee that every uncontrolled variable disappeared.\n\n26. A useful report separates what the route proved from why the team might choose it. Fast decoding may not compensate for excessive setup in short sessions, and a low-memory route may still be impractical if prompt processing is too slow. The final recommendation should describe these tradeoffs without inventing product requirements.\n\n27. The comparison also distinguishes artifact storage from runtime memory. Package bytes affect installation and download cost, mapped pages affect process accounting, and runtime caches grow with context and output. Reporting only the model file size would not answer whether the route fits comfortably on the phone.\n\n28. Each raw output is small enough to commit as text, unlike model weights and large binary profiles. Its file path, byte count, and SHA-256 bind later observations to exact bytes. If someone checks output sanity later, the registry can add that exact-hash observation without changing the original performance measurements.\n\n29. The benchmark should remain usable across CPU, GPU, and NPU routes. It therefore defines external timing and evidence boundaries without choosing an accelerator in advance. Provider-specific proof belongs to the experiment and must support, rather than replace, the common setup and request measurements.\n\n30. The immediate decision is which routes deserve deeper deployment work. The evidence should make blocked and negative results useful by preserving where they stopped, which tiers ran, and what remained unavailable. It should not turn a successful smoke test into a production-readiness claim.\n\nWrite a self-contained technical recommendation for the team. Explain the important tradeoffs, identify the measurements that should remain separate, and end with a practical next step. Use ordinary prose rather than a table.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n","has_new_line":true,"trun